---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: Interest costs
description: What the UK government pays to service its debt (yearly, monthly, and daily) and how that compares with revenue and the economy.
eleventyComputed:
  dataUpdated: "{{ [dashboard.lastUpdated, assumptions.budgetSimulator.lastUpdated] | latestOf }}"
---
{% from "components/macros.njk" import caveatBox, sourceNote %}

{% set interest = dashboard.metrics | findBy("id", "debt-interest") %}
{% set annual = interest.value %}
{% set revenue = assumptions.budgetSimulator.baselineGbpBillion.totalRevenue %}
{% set gdp = dashboard.supporting.gdp.value %}

<div class="page-intro prose">
  <h1>Interest costs</h1>
  <p class="lede">The government pays interest on its debt every day of the year. Here is what that costs, and why the rate matters as much as the size of the pile.</p>
</div>

<section aria-labelledby="breakdown">
  <h2 id="breakdown" class="visually-hidden">Interest cost breakdown</h2>
  <dl class="metric-grid metric-grid--compact">
    <div class="metric-card metric-card--mini">
      <dt class="metric-card__label">Per year</dt>
      <dd class="metric-card__value">£{{ annual | number }} bn</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card metric-card--mini">
      <dt class="metric-card__label">Per month (average)</dt>
      <dd class="metric-card__value">£{{ (annual / 12) | oneDp }} bn</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card metric-card--mini">
      <dt class="metric-card__label">Per day (average)</dt>
      <dd class="metric-card__value">£{{ (annual * 1000 / 365) | round(0) | number }} m</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card metric-card--mini">
      <dt class="metric-card__label">Of government revenue</dt>
      <dd class="metric-card__value">{{ (annual / revenue * 100) | round(0) }}%</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card metric-card--mini">
      <dt class="metric-card__label">Of GDP</dt>
      <dd class="metric-card__value">{{ (annual / gdp * 100) | oneDp }}%</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  {{ sourceNote(interest, "Monthly and daily are simple averages of the annual figure, which is the OBR's " + interest.period + " forecast. Per cent of revenue is calculated against the site's " + spendingComparisons.fiscalYear + " revenue figure, and per cent of GDP against the GDP consistent with the latest debt ratio.") }}
</section>

<div class="prose">
  <h2 id="why-rates">Why the interest rate matters as much as the debt</h2>
  <p>The same pile of debt can be cheap or expensive to hold, depending on the interest rate paid on it. Two things pushed UK debt interest up after 2021. High inflation raised the cost of <strong>index-linked gilts</strong>, whose value rises with prices. Higher Bank of England rates raised the cost of new and refinanced borrowing.</p>
  <p>So the cost can rise even when the debt itself is flat.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="trend">Historical trend</h2>
<details class="data-table-wrap" open>
  <summary>Annual debt interest over time</summary>
  <table class="data-table">
    <caption>Annual debt interest, £ billion (nominal). Each row carries its own confidence level; the last row is the OBR's forecast for the current financial year.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col" class="num">Debt interest (£ bn)</th><th scope="col">Confidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {%- for d in debtTimeseries.series %}
        {%- if d.interestGbp !== null %}
        <tr><th scope="row">{{ d.year }}</th><td class="num">{{ d.interestGbp | number }}</td><td>{{ d.confidence_level }}</td></tr>
        {%- endif %}
      {%- endfor %}
      <tr><th scope="row">{{ interest.period }} (OBR forecast)</th><td class="num">{{ annual | number }}</td><td>{{ interest.confidence_level }}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</details>
{{ sourceNote({ source_name: debtTimeseries.source_name, source_url: debtTimeseries.source_url, retrieved_date: debtTimeseries.retrieved_date }, "The " + interest.period + " row is from <a href=\"" + interest.source_url + "\" rel=\"noopener\">" + interest.source_name + "</a>.") }}

{{ caveatBox("Monthly and daily figures are simple averages for illustration. Actual interest is uneven, with large peaks when index-linked gilts are uprated. Figures are provisional and may be revised.", "How to read these") }}
